            <h2>Poetry</h2>

            <p>Poetry does what philosophy cannot: it bypasses the arguing mind and lands in the body. These poems are chosen because they enact Arbora's values rather than merely describing them.</p>

            <h3 id="basho">Matsuo Basho</h3>
            <p><em>The great haiku master. Three lines, seventeen syllables, the whole world.</em></p>
            <blockquote>"The old pond —<br>a frog jumps in,<br>sound of water."</blockquote>
            <blockquote>"Even in Kyoto —<br>hearing the cuckoo's cry —<br>I long for Kyoto."</blockquote>
            <blockquote>"Nothing in the cry<br>of cicadas suggests<br>they are about to die."</blockquote>

            <h3 id="oliver">Mary Oliver, "The Summer Day"</h3>
            <p><em>A poem about attention and the preciousness of life — the same questions that Ecclesiastes and the Stoics asked, rendered in a contemporary American voice.</em></p>
            <blockquote>"I don't know exactly what a prayer is.<br>I do know how to pay attention, how to fall down<br>into the grass, how to kneel down in the grass,<br>how to be idle and blessed, how to stroll through the fields,<br>which is what I have been doing all day.<br>Tell me, what else should I have done?<br>Doesn't everything die at last, and too soon?<br>Tell me, what is it you plan to do<br>with your one wild and precious life?"</blockquote>

            <h3 id="berry">Wendell Berry, "The Peace of Wild Things"</h3>
            <p><em>On what the living world offers when anxiety overwhelms.</em></p>
            <blockquote>"When despair for the world grows in me<br>and I wake in the night at the least sound<br>in fear of what my life and my children's lives may be,<br>I go and lie down where the wood drake<br>rests in his beauty on the water, and the great heron feeds.<br>I come into the peace of wild things<br>who do not tax their lives with forethought<br>of grief. I come into the presence of still water.<br>And I feel above me the day-blind stars<br>waiting with their light. For a time<br>I rest in the grace of the world, and am free."</blockquote>

            <h3 id="snyder">Gary Snyder, from "For the Children"</h3>
            <p><em>The poet of wild mind and ecological consciousness. This is deep time as parenting advice.</em></p>
            <blockquote>"stay together<br>learn the flowers<br>go light"</blockquote>

            <h3 id="jeffers">Robinson Jeffers, from "Carmel Point"</h3>
            <p><em>Jeffers' philosophy of "inhumanism" — the recognition that beauty and meaning exist beyond the human scale.</em></p>
            <blockquote>"As for us:<br>We must uncenter our minds from ourselves;<br>We must unhumanize our views a little, and become confident<br>As the rock and ocean that we were made from."</blockquote>

            <h3 id="amichai">Yehuda Amichai, "The Place Where We Are Right"</h3>
            <p><em>Israel's greatest modern poet. This poem is Arbora's seventh principle — question everything — rendered as image.</em></p>
            <blockquote>"From the place where we are right<br>flowers will never grow<br>in the spring.<br><br>The place where we are right<br>is hard and trampled<br>like a yard.<br><br>But doubts and loves<br>dig up the world<br>like a mole, a plow.<br>And a whisper will be heard in the place<br>where the ruined<br>house once stood."<br>— (trans. Stephen Mitchell)</blockquote>

            <h3 id="kabir">Kabir</h3>
            <p><em>A 15th-century Indian mystic poet who belonged to no religion and all of them. He wove Hindu and Muslim imagery into poems of radical non-duality.</em></p>
            <blockquote>"Are you looking for me? I am in the next seat.<br>My shoulder is against yours.<br>You will not find me in stupas, not in Indian shrine rooms,<br>nor in synagogues, nor in cathedrals:<br>not in masses, nor kirtans, not in legs winding<br>around your own neck, nor in eating nothing but vegetables.<br>When you really look for me, you will see me instantly —<br>you will find me in the tiniest house of time.<br>Kabir says: Student, tell me, what is God?<br>He is the breath inside the breath."<br>— (trans. Robert Bly)</blockquote>
